      "title": "Input Text (Prompt)",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfy-core",
        "ver": "0.29.0",
        "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "Dynamic collage-style intro sequence using four reference cover images as the core visual material. Each cover appears as an independent content panel, then transitions through split-screen, tile motion, sliding masks, panel expansion, and recombination into a final unified composite poster. The tone is energetic, professional, and visually rich, like a polished knowledge-channel opener. Keep the covers readable and recognizable. Emphasize motion design, modular panel animation, segmented layouts, and rhythmic assembly. Style should feel like a modern motion-graphics intro with layered 2D panel animation, clean compositing, strong visual hierarchy, and smooth but punchy timing.\n\nThe four covers are:\n1. A green-beige cover about image model LoRA training.\n2. A beige-orange cover about Boogu image Edit and removing text from images.\n3. A blue cover about Bernini reference-image video editing.\n4. A yellow cover about Ideogram V4 JSON-controlled text-to-image.\n\nTimeline:\n[0s-1.5s] Fast opening. The screen starts with a dark or lightly textured clean background. Thin graphic frames and guide lines appear quickly. The four cover panels flash in as cropped fragments from different directions, not yet fully visible. Small motion accents, snap-in movements, and quick panel alignment establish the rhythm.\n\n[1.5s-4s] The first full panel settles into view: the LoRA training cover. It appears slightly larger than the others, as if introducing the series. Subtle scale-up, light bounce, and a few supporting cropped pieces slide in around it. Then the frame splits horizontally or vertically, revealing part of the Boogu image Edit cover beside it.\n\n[4s-6.5s] The composition becomes a two-panel layout, then a three-panel layout. The Boogu image Edit cover expands into place, followed by the Bernini video-editing cover appearing through a sliding mask or tile-flip transition. Each cover should remain visually clear and readable, with a sense of structured modular design. Motion is fast but not messy.\n\n[6.5s-9s] All four covers now appear in a grid-like collage. The Ideogram V4 cover snaps into the remaining space. The screen divides into multiple clean rectangular blocks. Some blocks briefly zoom into key regions of the covers — titles,人物, UI cards, highlighted topic areas — then slide back into the collage. The layout should feel like a kinetic mosaic of tutorial content.\n\n[9s-11.5s] The collage begins a more advanced reconstruction phase. Panels split into smaller tiles, then rearrange. Some sections slide outward while others rotate slightly or wipe across the frame. The four covers dynamically trade positions through modular motion-graphics animation. The movement should suggest “many contents, one channel, one system.”\n\n[11.5s-13.5s] All moving pieces begin to converge. The tiles merge into one strong final composite poster: a unified cover made from the four original covers, arranged in a balanced, poster-like layout. The final composition should feel intentional, not chaotic — like a master key visual combining all four themes.\n\n[13.5s-15s] Final hold. The completed collage poster stays centered on screen with a subtle push-in or breathing motion. Light graphic accents settle. The final frame should feel like a strong channel opener or topic-summary visual, clean and complete.\n\nMotion language:\nUse panel sliding, masked reveals, crop-window animation, segmented split-screen, modular tile recombination, snap transitions, staggered motion, and layered collage movement. Emphasize structured motion design rather than random chaos. Movements should feel clean, graphic, and editorial. Allow brief zoom-ins on key parts of each cover, but always return to a strong overall layout.\n\nCamera:\nMostly 2D motion-graphics style camera behavior. Use subtle push-ins, slight reframing, and gentle parallax if needed. No aggressive handheld motion, no chaotic spinning, no cinematic live-action camera shake. The focus is on graphic layout animation and panel choreography.\n\nTransitions:\nHard cuts, sliding wipes, tile reveals, split-frame expansion, directional panel movement, and clean snap-ins. No soft dissolves. No blurry morphing. No overly complex 3D rotations. Motion should feel crisp and deliberate.\n\nAudio:\nUpbeat motion-design intro music with a modern digital rhythm, punchy beats, soft synth layers, and a confident knowledge-channel vibe. Add synchronized UI-like motion sound effects: whooshes, panel swipes, light clicks, snap transitions, tile assembly sounds, and subtle impact hits when major panels lock into place. The final merge should have a satisfying assembly sound and a short resolving hit at the end.\n\nOptional title behavior:\nIf text is added, keep it minimal and strong, appearing only near the end or integrated briefly during transitions. Text should support the intro rather than cover the thumbnails. All visible Chinese text already present in the cover images must remain clear and legible.\n\nConstraints:\nPreserve the identity and main layout of all four reference covers.\nDo not replace the covers with unrelated new artwork.\nKeep the collage clean, readable, and visually organized.\nDo not over-blur or heavily distort the cover text.\nNo subtitle bars.\nNo excessive camera shake.\nNo messy particle overload.\nNo soft dissolves.\nNo random background objects unrelated to the four covers.\nThe final 15-second video should feel like a polished intro opener made from four educational thumbnail covers, using dynamic collage and segmented panel animation."
      ],
